<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sunassura</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1400 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a> · Also known as Shunashshura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sunassura was a king of Kizzuwatna in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> around 1400 BCE. A treaty with the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-i">Tudhaliya I</a> records that he left the side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni Empire</a> and accepted Hittite overlordship, with detailed terms on the borders and duties of the two kingdoms. Soon afterwards Kizzuwatna was absorbed into the Hittite state, and its cults passed to the Hittite court.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Before Sunassura, under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pilliya">Pilliya</a>, Kizzuwatna stood under the overlordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>. The kingdom mattered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> because it lay on the route connecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. A dispute between Sunassura and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niqmepa-of-alalakh">Niqmepa of Alalakh</a> was settled by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaushtatar">Shaushtatar</a> of Mitanni, a judgement recorded on two tablets, AIT 13 and AIT 14, found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Sunassura concluded a treaty with the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-i">Tudhaliya I</a>, which marked the end of Kizzuwatna's independence; the kingdom was afterwards integrated into the Hittite state. The treaty fixed the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urshu">Urshu</a> as the eastern frontier of Kizzuwatna. Several copies of treaties between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a> and Sunassura survive, some in Akkadian and some in Hittite. The Hittite version exists in two copies, A and B, which seem to record two different treaties; copy B, which gave Kizzuwatna better terms, is probably the earlier.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The differences between the three texts have led some scholars to propose two separate, successive treaties. Jacques Freu sees in them first an equal and then an unequal arrangement made under two different pairs of kings, and so distinguishes two Hittite great kings named Tudhaliya. Gary Beckman instead holds that a single Hittite king Tudhaliya reigned for a long time and dealt with two successive kings of Kizzuwatna, Sunassura I and Sunassura II.</p><p>Those who accept a second Sunassura place him later, at the time of Tudhaliya II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaushtatar">Shaushtatar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, when Mitanni and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a> were in conflict, and see him as the last independent ruler of Kizzuwatna; it was perhaps then that Tudhaliya II made an unequal treaty with him, recalling the country's earlier treaty under his grandfather. In a dissertation of 2011 Boaz Stavi considered whether relations between Hatti and Mitanni were close under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-i">Tudhaliya I</a>, so that Kizzuwatna's real conflict with Hatti began only at the end of that reign, under Arnuwanda I or early under Tudhaliya II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sunassura” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its kings, such as Isputahsu, Pilliya and <b>Sunassura</b>, concluded treaties with Hittite rulers and with Idrimi of Mukish.</blockquote><a href="/state/kizzuwatna">Kizzuwatna</a></li></ul></section>
